::Q1::What is a "unit" in the context of Python unit testing?
{ 
~A complete application that can be tested only as a whole
~A collection of several programs that share a database
=The smallest piece of code that can be tested on its own, usually a function or method
~A manual checklist used during software development
}

::Q2::Which type of testing checks that multiple units work correctly together? 
{
=Integration testing
~Unit testing
~Syntax testing
~Manual testing
}

::Q3::A function produces the same result every time it receives the same input. Which testing characteristic does this demonstrate? 
{
~Randomized behavior
~Manual execution
~Integration dependence
=Predictable behavior
}

::Q4::What happens when a Python assert condition evaluates to false? 
{
~Python automatically fixes the tested function
~The assertion is silently ignored
~Python raises a SyntaxError
=Python raises an AssertionError
}

::Q5::Why are automated tests useful as a codebase grows? 
{
~They guarantee that every program is free of bugs
~They eliminate the need to write expected behavior
~They make integration testing unnecessary
=They provide repeatable checks that can be run quickly after changes
}